specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:39:- Importing the package root MUST NOT require either optional peer to be installed. Measured on the built `dist/`: a bare root import loaded 38 `@google-cloud/vertexai` modules before this change and 0 after, with Bedrock at 0 throughout. The `full` smoke variant holds that number: it is the only place both peers are installed, so it is the only place a barrel that went eager again would be visible at all, and it counts the SDK modules loaded by the root import rather than asserting a directory is absent.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:69:`@types/express`, `@types/node` and `@types/ws` move from `devDependencies` to `dependencies`. The emitted `.d.ts` files import `express`, `http`, `stream` and `ws` unconditionally — `types/auth.d.ts` and three files under `transport/` — so with those packages dev-only a consumer resolves the runtime fine and fails to type-check. Measured on the packed tarball in an empty project: eight `TS7016`/`TS2591`/`TS2307` errors before the move, and a clean `tsc` exit after it. This is a manifest defect rather than a smoke-test finding, which is why it lands with the manifest; the smoke test is what stops it returning.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:105:AGENTS.md § Quality Gates already commits this repository to "npm audit must show no vulnerabilities", and nothing enforced it. Measured before this work: 18 advisories — 1 critical, 6 high, 8 moderate, 3 low — and `ws`, a direct production dependency, was one of the six high.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:107:- A check fails on any unacknowledged advisory at `high` or above, over the full installed tree rather than production dependencies only. The dev tree is what a runner installs before building `dist/`, and after the release workflow exists that runner holds `id-token: write`.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:110:- Both checks run as their own CI job rather than as steps on the existing one, because they report on what the build chain pulls in rather than on what the code does, and again before `npm publish`.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:118:The gate runs immediately before `npm publish`, so the two ways it can report a clean tree without having checked one are part of its contract rather than details of its implementation. It shells out to `npm audit`, whose registry failures are themselves JSON and carry no `vulnerabilities` key; and an acceptance names an advisory, which means an acceptance recorded for one advisory must not absorb the next one in the same package.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:152:The trust-boundary check runs before the gates rather than after them. A tag pushed from a branch that never reached `main` is rejected in seconds instead of after a full build and test run.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:170:- It runs as its own CI job, and T011 calls it in the publish workflow before `npm publish`.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:171:- The check is load-bearing rather than decorative: reverting the T004 dependency move reproduces 8 `tsc` errors, and restoring the top-level Vertex import from before T002 reproduces `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` — both caught by this script alone.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:192:- Publishing a GitHub Release triggers one publish job. The release carries the tag and the notes; the version is the one already committed in `package.json`, so a release names a version a pull request agreed to rather than minting one at the point of no return. A draft release fires nothing, and one ticked as a pre-release is refused by the job condition before a runner starts.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:200:- A guard fails the job when the tag and the committed `package.json` version disagree. It is a committed script rather than an inline step, so a developer can run it before pushing a tag they cannot un-push ([validated by: refuses a tag that disagrees with the committed version](../../scripts/check-version.test.ts#L36)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:245:- The `v0.2.0` tag is pushed after the bootstrap, on the commit it published, and no release object is created for it. A tag with no release still renders at `/releases/tag/v0.2.0`, which is what the changelog links to, and the publish workflow no longer triggers on a tag push - so the link resolves and nothing runs. Creating a release instead would fire `publish.yml`, pass every gate against the tagged commit and fail at the staging step against a version that already exists: a permanently red run in the history, bought for a row on the releases page. This replaces the earlier plan to accept that red run, which predates the release-event trigger. The attestation on `0.2.0` names the bootstrap workflow, which no longer exists in the tree, and `npm audit signatures` still verifies it, because the attestation records the workflow at the commit it ran from.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:290:- It is invoked after the `connected` frame is sent, not before ([validated by: runs after the connected frame is sent, not before it](../../src/transport/ws/connectionHandler.test.ts#L82)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:291:- It is not invoked inline in the `connection` listener, where a synchronous throw corrupts an already-upgraded socket. It runs on a microtask, which drains before the loop delivers any inbound frame ([validated by: is deferred, so it never runs inside the connection listener](../../src/transport/ws/connectionHandler.test.ts#L94)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:311:- A level below the `LOG_LEVEL` threshold is dropped before the line is built ([validated by: drops a level below the configured threshold before writing anything](../../src/shared/logger.test.ts#L98)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:330:Delegating to a supplied logger initially bypassed both of the built-in emitter's guarantees, because both lived in the emitter rather than in the dispatch above it. The level test and the throw guard now sit in one place, applied before the active logger is called, so they hold whichever logger is installed.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:347:Measured before the fix, one config object produced `{"status":"","count":0}` through `createProvider` and `{"status":"configured","count":42}` through `createMockProvider`.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:354:- The mock is the only provider the tarball smoke test can exercise without a cloud account, which is why this lands before the smoke fixture is written.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:365:- No deprecation window was needed. This package has never been published, so there were no registry consumers to deprecate for, and the window would only ever have been cheap before the first release.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:377:Removing that listener once `listen` succeeded left the running server with none, and an `EventEmitter` with no `error` listener throws - so a socket error on a started server ended the process. A persistent listener replaces it, and its lifetime is the part worth stating: it is removed before each bind attempt, and never on `stop()`.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:382:- A bind failure is not reported as a running server's error: the stale listener is removed before each attempt, so a refused `start()` rejects and logs nothing ([validated by: is not reported for a start that was cleanly refused, because nothing was running](../../src/transport/createServer.test.ts#L127)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:384:- A second `start()` on a running server is refused before the handlers are touched, so the running server keeps its handler. Node's `listen` throws on a listening server, and the swap above had already removed the persistent listener by then, leaving the next socket error unhandled ([validated by: refuses a second start without stripping the running server of its handler](../../src/transport/createServer.test.ts#L149)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:404:- The intake is `security@re-cinq.com` with a 48-hour acknowledgement target. Two facts belong with it rather than in the policy: the acknowledgement target is a commitment somebody honours at a weekend, and nothing here verifies the mailbox is monitored. Confirm both before the first publish - a policy naming an unread address converts a reporter who would have found you into one who thinks they told you.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:428:- The mechanism covers ten documents and 62 blocks: fifteen from the first pass and 47 more across `CLAUDE.md`, `.specify/spec.md`, the three how-tos, and the providers and tool-responses specs. Fifteen are generated from a declaration or an example region; the rest carry a reasoned opt-out naming what the block actually is - an invented type a how-to uses to demonstrate its steps, a before-and-after pair, a provider a reader writes.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:442:- `specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md` § 8.1 is the full-conversation example every consumer reads first, and it shows the thinking entry's commit frame before the tool upsert frame. The shipped code emits them the other way round: a thinking entry commits when the next text segment arrives, and a tool-use chunk commits nothing. A client written from the example waits for a commit that comes later than documented and renders nothing in exactly the window a tool call should fill. Corrected, and pinned: the tool upsert now precedes the thinking commit, and a new test drives the real handler through thinking, tool and answer so the documented sequence is the emitted one. The two were compared programmatically rather than by eye, and match frame for frame.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:443:- §8.1 also gains a statement that commit frames are not emitted in index order, which is the general rule the corrected example is one instance of. A client assuming ascending commits, or that an entry commits before the next opens, mis-renders the exchange.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:473:- Every `*.test.ts` file cites a spec statement. Every new test written for this work needs a statement in some spec to cite, written before or with it.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:481:- Whether the repository becomes public is [ADR-007](../../adrs/ADR-007-repository-visibility.md), stated generally enough that the next package reads it rather than re-deriving it. It decided the `--provenance` question the release workflow could not be finished without. The trade-off is narrower than it first appears: the built package is public either way, so what visibility decides is the history, the test suite, the examples, and the issue and pull-request record — and exposure covers every commit, not the tip, so it requires a full-history secret scan first.
specs/hal-engine-mongo-session-store/spec.md:16:- `evict` removes the document as well as the cache entry: the engine calls it only for a session whose socket closed before the client received it, so its document holds no entries and leaving one behind would accumulate conversations nobody can read ([validated by: leaves no document behind when a session the client never received is evicted](../../src/infrastructure/stores/mongo/mongoDurability.test.ts#L103)).
specs/hal-engine-mongo-session-store/spec.md:26:- `eraseOlderThan(cutoff)` removes every document whose `createdAt` precedes the cutoff and no others, asserted at the boundary with one document a second either side ([validated by: erases only what was created strictly before the cutoff](../../src/infrastructure/stores/mongo/mongoDurability.test.ts#L58)).
specs/hal-engine-doc-gates/spec.md:8:Four rules about the documents this repository publishes, each of which held only because somebody remembered it. A code block that matches its source, a path in prose that resolves, a spike that says it is a spike, and an example region inside the EU. Every one of them had already been broken at least once before it was a gate: hand transcription put a wrong frame order, a field name that never existed and three unread config keys into shipped documents, and a Vertex snippet handed a reader a US region to copy.
specs/hal-engine-spec-status/spec.md:37:- A spec that opens straight into a section, with no lead paragraph before the first `##`, is reported against line 1 ([validated by: a spec opening straight into a section fails with re-lint/require-intro-paragraph at line 1](../../scripts/eslint-spec-docs.test.ts#L35)).
specs/hal-engine-session-lifetime/spec.md:19:- A session whose socket closed before it was delivered has its credentials cleared too, because `evict` is optional and a store that implements none still holds it ([validated by: clears the credentials of a session whose socket closed before it was delivered](../../src/transport/ws/connectionHandler.test.ts#L273)).
specs/hal-engine-session-lifetime/spec.md:21:- A session created for a socket that dies before the `connected` frame can be written is evicted rather than left behind, so a failed handover leaves no conversation nobody will ever read ([validated by: evicts a session it created but could not hand over](../../src/transport/ws/connectionHandler.test.ts#L333)).
specs/hal-engine-session-lifetime/spec.md:36:The clearing is an in-memory measure, and deliberately not a store call. `authHeaders` is transport state rather than conversation content, and no store should persist it at all: a store holds credentials only if it wrote them itself, and it wrote them during a turn, long before any close event exists — so an engine-side redaction on close could only chase what is already on disk, and would miss a process that crashed rather than closed. The guarantee has to be "never written" rather than "written, then erased", which puts it in the store's write path rather than here. Calling `save` on close is not the alternative it appears to be either: the write signal is specified to fire exactly once per processed user message, and firing it again would make a disconnect look like a turn.
specs/hal-engine-spec-anchor-drift/spec.md:91:The gate has a blind spot worth knowing. Specs that do not exist on `origin/main` yet — every spec on the branch that introduced them — are skipped for the baseline comparison, so on such a branch only the rotten check is really running. Staleness in a new spec is caught from the first merge onward, not before.
specs/hal-engine-subprotocol-marker/spec.md:13:- The server answers `hal.v1` whenever it is offered, whether before the token or after it ([validated by: answers the marker when it is offered first, never the token beside it](../../src/transport/ws/subprotocol.test.ts#L23), [validated by: answers the marker when it is offered after the token](../../src/transport/ws/subprotocol.test.ts#L27)).
specs/hal-engine-subprotocol-marker/spec.md:20:- It is forwarded to the session as `Bearer <token>` in `authHeaders.authorization`, as before ([validated by: answers a hal.v1 and token offer with hal.v1 alone, and forwards the token as a bearer](../../src/transport/handshake.test.ts#L127)).
specs/hal-engine-subprotocol-marker/spec.md:21:- A client offering the marker and two other values gets the first of them, matching the behaviour before the marker ([validated by: reads the first non-marker value when two are offered beside the marker](../../src/transport/ws/subprotocol.test.ts#L49)).
specs/hal-engine-subprotocol-marker/spec.md:30:- Behind a `WsAuthenticator` that reads its credential with `credentialFromSubprotocol`, a real bare-token client finds no credential and is refused with `401` before any upgrade ([validated by: refuses a bare-token offer with 401 when the authenticator reads its credential with credentialFromSubprotocol](../../src/transport/handshake.test.ts#L153)).
specs/hal-engine-providers/spec.md:12:Model ids below were checked against vendor sources on 2026-09-11, by documentation and by a live publisher-model listing for Vertex. Ids are vendor-controlled and retire on the vendor's schedule, not this package's: `modelId` is a bare unvalidated string on both working providers, so a retired id reaches a consumer as an error that looks like their credentials are wrong. Re-check before trusting a snippet that is older than a few months.
specs/hal-engine-providers/spec.md:168:- Such a turn used to end `end_turn`, which contradicted the `tool_use` chunk before it, and the tool loop, the field's only reader, stopped there: on Vertex no tool ran, and the turn ended with no answer. Driven through the orchestrator, a function call now runs its registered tool and the next request carries its result as a `functionResponse` named after the function ([validated by: closes the tool loop on its own chunks: the call runs its tool and the next request carries the result](../../src/vertexToolLoop.test.ts#L33)).
specs/hal-engine-providers/spec.md:278:`SendMessageParams.signal` is how the engine tells a provider to stop. The field is optional, so a provider written before it existed still compiles and behaves as it did, and `withRetry` passes one on every attempt (specs/hal-engine-architecture/spec.md § Provider resilience). Once the signal aborts, a provider yields nothing more and ends its stream without raising: an abort is the engine's own decision, not a vendor failure, and an `AIError` would reach the client as an error frame it did not earn. What an abort stops at the vendor depends on its SDK; [docs/implementing-a-provider.md](../../docs/implementing-a-provider.md) § Cancellation records it per provider and SDK version, with what that means for where personal data goes.
specs/hal-engine-providers/spec.md:283:- With no signal, `client.send` receives the command alone, exactly as before the field existed ([validated by: calls client.send with the command alone when no signal is given](../../src/providers/bedrock/bedrockProvider.test.ts#L65)).
specs/hal-engine-providers/spec.md:293:- The SDK fills a `response` promise from a copy of the stream, and that promise is given a rejection handler before the stream is read. Unobserved, a stream that failed mid-way left it rejected with nothing handling it, and Node ended the process although the engine had caught the same failure from the stream ([validated by: observes the SDK response promise, so its rejection never becomes an unhandled rejection](../../src/providers/vertex/vertexProvider.test.ts#L224)).
specs/hal-engine-thinking-tag-parser/spec.md:23:- A complete pair splits into the text before it, the thinking between, and the text after ([validated by: splits text, thinking and trailing text around a complete tag pair](../../src/infrastructure/parsers/thinkingTagParser.test.ts#L42)).
specs/hal-engine-thinking-tag-parser/spec.md:35:The tail of the buffer is withheld while it is still a prefix of the tag being looked for; everything before that tail is emitted immediately.
specs/hal-engine-thinking-tag-parser/spec.md:37:- A chunk ending in a partial opening tag emits the text before it and holds the rest ([validated by: withholds the part that could still become an opening tag](../../src/infrastructure/parsers/thinkingTagParser.test.ts#L77)).
specs/hal-engine-thinking-tag-parser/spec.md:40:- Inside a thinking block the same algorithm runs against the closing tag, with `thinking` as the segment type: a chunk ending in a partial closing tag emits the thinking before it and holds the rest ([validated by: withholds the part that could still become a closing tag](../../src/infrastructure/parsers/thinkingTagParser.test.ts#L101)).
